            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nThis text is a simple introduction to the multiplication tables of zero and one. We recommend reading *Adding the same numbers* (<htcite0>) first, where you will learn that multiplying is a way of adding the same number many times.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "d862242c-0538-4b18-8b32-4a84d4a5858e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Adding the same numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nA table is like a map of answers. When we know the map, we do not need to count everything again. Today we will learn two very special tables: the table of zero (0) and the table of one (1).",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Before we begin, remember that multiplying is a fast way of adding the same number many times.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_zero",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Multiplying by Zero (0)\n\nZero means \"nothing\" (<htcite0>). As a consequence of this, zero is not simply an empty place: it is the number that tells us there is no quantity to count. When we say that there are zero objects, we are not saying that we do not know how many there are; we are saying that there are none at all. No matter how many groups we form, if every group has nothing, the total remains nothing. This is why multiplying by zero always gives zero.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "576e4648-77fa-4a6d-9e51-9459ced964f3",
                     "text": "Musa, Auwalu",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "19"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_lab_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_lab question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-microscope\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_lab\"><b>Practice 1: Plates with no cookies</b><br />Imagine that we do not have any cookies, but we still want to put cookies on a plate, as shown in Figure 1. If we imagine 4 plates, 8 plates or any other number of plates, each one receives 0 cookies, because there are no cookies to put on them: 4 × 0 = 0, 8 × 0 = 0. No matter how many plates we imagine, the result is always zero, since we have no cookies.</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 240\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:240px; display:block; margin:0 auto;\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"An empty plate\"><title>An empty plate</title><ellipse cx=\"120\" cy=\"222\" rx=\"88\" ry=\"12\" fill=\"#000000\" opacity=\"0.12\"/><circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"116\" r=\"100\" fill=\"#f7f7f7\" stroke=\"#c9c9c9\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"116\" r=\"76\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#dcdcdc\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"116\" r=\"60\" fill=\"#fdfdfd\" stroke=\"#e6e6e6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><ellipse cx=\"86\" cy=\"82\" rx=\"26\" ry=\"13\" fill=\"#ffffff\" opacity=\"0.8\" transform=\"rotate(-25 86 82)\"/></svg><b>Figure 1</b>: An empty plate.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "No matter how many plates we have, if every plate is empty, there are no cookies.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Empty groups</b><br /><br />When there is nothing to count, will the result always be zero?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "When we multiply a number by zero, the answer is always zero. This works for people, trees, cookies, or anything else.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "We can write this rule as a formula:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p class=\"center_notation\"><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>0</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>0</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mi>N</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 0</b>: Any number multiplied by zero is zero.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- The **result** (left side) is the product.\n- The **factors** are on the right side.\n- **N** can be any number.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Hover the mouse over the multiplications on the left to visualize each one in the table.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div id=\"mParent0\" style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_one",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Multiplying by One (1)\n\nNow let's learn what happens when we multiply by one (1).\n\n#### Counting on our fingers\n\nA simple way to understand multiplication by one is to count on your fingers. Use the arrows in Table 1 to see this in action:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"tawapukllay\" id=\"tblHandsCounting\"><tr><th>Left Hand</th><th>Right Hand</th><th>Arrows</th></tr> <tr id=\"tr0\"><td><img id=\"lefthand\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('lefthand', '0%')\" /></td><td><img id=\"righthand\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('righthand', '0%')\" /></td><td style=\"background-color: white;\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-caret-up upArrowWithFA\" id=\"traineeUp0\"></i>  <i class=\"fa-solid fa-caret-down downArrowWithFA\"style=\"font-size:4em;color:#00f;\" id=\"traineeDown0\"></td></tr> </table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 1</b>: Examples of counting and multiplication by 1.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Multiplying a number by one means adding one repeatedly until you reach that number. For example, 3 × 1 means 1 + 1 + 1 = 3. The answer is always the same number.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "We can write this rule as a formula:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mi>N</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 1</b>: Any number multiplied by one is the same number.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Look at Table 1: when the arrow points to 4, we have 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 4, and 4 × 1 = 4.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Just as we did for 0, you can also hover the mouse over the multiplications on the left to visualize each one in the table.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div id=\"mParent1\" style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nIn this text we learned two special rules:\n\n- Any number multiplied by zero gives zero: 0 = 0 × N.\n- Any number multiplied by one stays the same: N = 1 × N.\n\nTry these rules with numbers you know. You will see that they work every time.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      }
   ],
   "exercise_v2": [
      {
         "question": "If we have 3 empty boxes, is the total number of toys inside them zero?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "Yes",
         "additionalInfo": "0 + 0 + 0 = 0, so 3 × 0 = 0."
      },
      {
         "question": "Does multiplying a number by one change the number?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "No",
         "additionalInfo": "Multiplying by one keeps the same number: 5 × 1 = 5."
      },
      {
         "question": "Is 7 × 0 equal to 7?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "No",
         "additionalInfo": "Any number multiplied by zero is zero: 7 × 0 = 0."
      }
   ],
